sections.s2_technical:
REST organises the whole API around resources. A resource is any meaningful business thing: an account, a transfer, a beneficiary. Each resource has a stable URL, called a URI, and you act on it using HTTP methods. For example, reading Rajesh's account is `GET /api/v1/accounts/SAV2001`, and that URL identifies the resource while GET identifies the action.

The first constraint is client-server. The client (a mobile app) and the server (the banking service) are separate and evolve independently. The client only needs to know the uniform interface, not how the server stores data.

The second constraint is statelessness. Every request from the client must carry everything the server needs to process it, including authentication. The server keeps no session memory between requests. For example, the JWT token is sent on every call: `GET /accounts/SAV2001` with header `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. This matters because any server instance can handle any request, so you can scale horizontally.

The third constraint is cacheability. Responses must say whether they can be cached. A balance lookup might include `Cache-Control: max-age=30`, telling clients and proxies they may reuse the answer for thirty seconds.

The fourth constraint is a layered system. The client cannot tell whether it is talking to the real server or an intermediary like a load balancer, gateway, or cache. This lets banks put security and caching layers in front without the client caring.

The fifth constraint is the uniform interface — the heart of REST. It means resource identification in URLs, manipulation through representations (you send JSON, the server stores it however it wants), self-descriptive messages (the `Content-Type` header tells you the body is `application/json`), and HATEOAS.

The sixth constraint, optional, is code-on-demand, where the server can send executable code like JavaScript. Most APIs skip this.

HATEOAS means the response includes links telling the client what it can do next. A transfer response might include a link to cancel the transfer, so the client discovers actions instead of hardcoding URLs.

An RPC-style HTTP API breaks these rules. It uses action verbs in the URL like `POST /getUserById` or `POST /transferMoney`, treats HTTP as a dumb tunnel, usually returns 200 even for errors, and the client must know every custom endpoint name in advance. A RESTful API uses `GET /users/123` and `POST /transfers`, leaning on HTTP verbs and status codes that every tool already understands.

```java
// RESTful: noun + verb
// GET    /api/v1/accounts/SAV2001        read account
// POST   /api/v1/transfers               create a transfer
// DELETE /api/v1/beneficiaries/BEN-77    remove a beneficiary

// RPC-style (avoid):
// POST   /api/getAccount    body {"id":"SAV2001"}
// POST   /api/doTransfer    body {...}
```

A senior nuance: REST is not "JSON over HTTP." Many APIs calling themselves REST are actually RPC with pretty URLs. True REST leans on HTTP semantics — correct verbs, correct status codes, caching headers, and ideally hypermedia links.

