sections.s2_technical:
PUT replaces a resource at a known URL with the representation in the request body. The mental model is that the body you send becomes the complete new state of that resource. If Priya's beneficiary has four fields and you PUT a body with only three, the fourth is treated as absent and becomes null or default, because PUT means this is the whole thing now.

```java
// PUT: full replacement. Omitting ifsc means ifsc becomes null.
@PutMapping("/beneficiaries/{id}")
public Beneficiary replace(@PathVariable String id, @RequestBody BeneficiaryRequest body) {
    // build a fresh object from body; any field not in body is default/null
    return service.replace(id, body);
}
```

PUT is idempotent. Sending the same complete body twice ends in the same state both times, because the second PUT overwrites with identical values. This lets clients and proxies retry a PUT safely after a timeout.

PATCH applies a partial modification. The client sends only the fields it wants to change, and the server merges them into the existing resource. Fields not mentioned are left exactly as they were. This is the right verb when Priya wants to change only her nickname and keep the account number and IFSC.

```java
// PATCH: partial merge. Only non-null fields are applied.
@PatchMapping("/beneficiaries/{id}")
public Beneficiary patch(@PathVariable String id, @RequestBody PatchRequest body) {
    return service.patch(id, body); // fields left null in body are untouched
}
```

PATCH is not guaranteed idempotent. If the PATCH expresses a relative change like increment the balance by one hundred, running it twice adds two hundred. If the PATCH sets absolute values, it happens to be idempotent, but the specification does not require it, so you treat PATCH as non-idempotent unless you designed it otherwise.

There is a subtlety with PATCH and null. If Priya actually wants to clear a field with PATCH, a simple "ignore nulls" merge cannot express the difference between change this to null and do not touch this. That is one reason the JSON Patch format exists, which has explicit operations like remove, but a common simpler approach is JSON Merge Patch where an explicit null means delete the field.

Validation also differs. For PUT you validate the full object because every field must be present and valid. For PATCH you validate only the fields that are present, because the others are intentionally missing.

A senior nuance: many teams implement PATCH incorrectly by treating a missing field and an explicit null the same way, which quietly loses the ability to clear a field. The correct, careful implementations either adopt JSON Merge Patch semantics, where null means delete, or JSON Patch, where operations are explicit. For most internal banking APIs, the pragmatic choice is a merge where null means ignore, documented clearly so no one is surprised.

