sections.s2_technical:
The 2xx class means success. 200 OK is the general success for reads and updates that return a body. 201 Created is returned when a POST creates a new resource, usually with a Location header pointing to it, for example after creating a transfer. 202 Accepted means the request was received but processing happens later, useful for asynchronous jobs like a large batch payout. 204 No Content means success with no body, typical after a DELETE.

```java
// 201 Created with Location after a successful POST
return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/api/v1/transfers/" + id)).body(response);
```

The 3xx class means redirection. 301 Moved Permanently says the resource now lives at a new URL forever, and clients should update their links. 302 Found is a temporary redirect. 304 Not Modified tells the client its cached copy is still valid, so it should reuse it and save bandwidth; this is used with ETags and conditional requests.

The 4xx class means the client made a mistake. 400 Bad Request means the request was malformed, such as invalid JSON or a missing required field. 401 Unauthorized means the client is not authenticated, so no valid credentials were provided. 403 Forbidden means the client is authenticated but not allowed to do this action. 404 Not Found means the resource does not exist. 405 Method Not Allowed means the verb is wrong for this resource, like POST on a read-only endpoint. 409 Conflict means the request conflicts with the current state, such as creating a duplicate account. 422 Unprocessable Entity means the syntax was fine but the content failed business validation, like a negative transfer amount. 429 Too Many Requests means the client is rate limited.

```java
// 409 Conflict when the account already exists
if (service.exists(id)) {
    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CONFLICT).body("Account already exists");
}
```

The 5xx class means the server failed. 500 Internal Server Error is a generic server crash, something unexpected. 502 Bad Gateway means a server acting as a gateway got an invalid response from an upstream server. 503 Service Unavailable means the server is temporarily down or overloaded, often with a Retry-After header. 504 Gateway Timeout means an upstream server did not respond in time.

The 1xx class is informational and rarely seen directly in application code; 100 Continue is used during large uploads.

Choosing the right code matters because the whole ecosystem keys off it. Monitoring dashboards count 5xx as outages and 4xx as client issues. Retry logic retries 503 and 429 but not 400. Caches respect 304. If you return 200 for everything, as some RPC-style APIs do, you blind all of these tools and hide real failures.

A senior nuance: the difference between 400 and 422 is whether the request was syntactically malformed versus semantically invalid, and the difference between 401 and 403 is not authenticated versus not authorized. Getting these right makes client handling and monitoring far more precise, and interviewers probe these edges to see if you truly understand the semantics rather than just returning 200 and 500.

