sections.s2_technical:
Problem Details solves a real coordination problem. Before it, every team invented its own error JSON, so a client talking to five services had to learn five error shapes. RFC 9457 standardises the shape so one parser works everywhere, and because it is an IETF standard, tools, gateways, and libraries can understand it out of the box.

The media type is application/problem+json, which signals to the client that the body is a standard problem document and not a normal resource. This lets clients branch on content type.

The fields each have a defined role. The type is a URI, usually a stable URL like https://api.nbi.in/problems/insufficient-funds, and it is the field clients switch on, playing the same role as the stable machine code in a hand-rolled shape. The title is a short, human-readable summary of the problem type that should stay the same for a given type. The status repeats the HTTP status code in the body, which is convenient for logging. The detail is a human-readable explanation specific to this occurrence, like your balance is 250000 but you tried to transfer 900000. The instance is a URI identifying this specific occurrence, often a link to a log or a request id.

Spring Boot supports this natively. The ProblemDetail class represents the document, and you can create and enrich it easily:

```java
import org.springframework.http.ProblemDetail;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import java.net.URI;

ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(
        HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY, "Balance 250000 is less than requested 900000");
pd.setType(URI.create("https://api.nbi.in/problems/insufficient-funds")); // stable, switchable
pd.setTitle("Insufficient funds");
pd.setInstance(URI.create("/api/v1/transfers/req-abc123"));
pd.setProperty("traceId", "a1b2c3d4"); // extension member
```

You can return a ProblemDetail directly from a controller or an exception handler, and Spring sets the content type to application/problem+json automatically. The ErrorResponse interface and the ErrorResponseException let framework and custom exceptions carry a ProblemDetail, so Spring's own exceptions, like a 404 for a missing handler, can also produce problem documents when you enable it.

```java
@RestControllerAdvice
public class ProblemHandler {
    @ExceptionHandler(InsufficientFundsException.class)
    public ProblemDetail handle(InsufficientFundsException e) {
        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(
                HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY, e.getMessage());
        pd.setType(URI.create("https://api.nbi.in/problems/insufficient-funds"));
        pd.setTitle("Insufficient funds");
        pd.setProperty("traceId", MDC.get("traceId"));
        return pd; // Spring sets Content-Type: application/problem+json
    }
}
```

Why prefer it over a hand-rolled shape: it is a documented, framework-supported contract. New engineers already know it, API documentation tools render it, and clients across languages have libraries for it. A hand-rolled shape has to be invented, documented, and maintained, and every client must learn it separately. By adopting the standard you inherit a shared vocabulary and reduce bespoke work.

A senior nuance: the type URL being stable and switchable is the crux. Clients must branch on type, not on title or detail, because title and detail are human text that may change or be localised. The type URL does not even need to resolve to a live page, though it is good practice to make it a documentation link, but it must be a stable identifier that never changes once published, because it is part of your API contract exactly like your endpoint URLs.

