sections.s2_technical:
A DTO is a plain object with fields matching what a client needs, often a Java record for immutability. It is distinct from the entity, which is the JPA-managed object mapped to a database table. The DTO exists purely to move data across the boundary between internal code and the outside world.

Decoupling is the first reason. The entity reflects the database schema, which changes for internal reasons like adding an index column or splitting a table. If you expose the entity directly, every schema change risks breaking clients. A DTO is a stable contract: the entity can change while the DTO stays the same, and a mapping layer bridges them.

```java
// Entity: internal, mapped to DB, may hold sensitive fields.
@Entity class Account {
    @Id String id;
    String holderName;
    String passwordHash;   // NEVER sent to clients
    BigDecimal balance;
    int internalRiskScore; // internal only
}
// DTO: external contract, only safe fields.
record AccountResponse(String accountId, String holderName, BigDecimal balance) {}
```

Security is the second reason. Entities often carry sensitive fields like a password hash, an internal risk score, or audit metadata. Serialising the entity directly would leak these to the client. A response DTO includes only the fields a client may see, so secrets never cross the boundary by accident.

Data shaping is the third reason. A client screen may need a combination of fields from several entities, or computed values like an available balance that is balance minus holds. A DTO can flatten several entities into one object and include computed fields, giving the client exactly the shape it needs in one response.

Performance is the fourth reason. Entities may have large graphs of related data, and exposing them can trigger loading of relationships the client does not need, or serialise huge payloads. A DTO carries only the required fields, reducing payload size and avoiding unnecessary database loads. It also sidesteps lazy loading problems that occur when a serialiser touches an unloaded relationship outside a transaction.

API evolution is the fifth reason. With separate DTOs you can version the API, add a v2 response DTO with new fields, or deprecate a field, all without touching the entity or the database. The DTO is the unit of the public contract, so it evolves on the API's schedule, not the database's.

```java
// Mapping lives in the service layer, keeping controllers thin.
AccountResponse toResponse(Account a) {
    return new AccountResponse(a.getId(), a.getHolderName(), a.getBalance());
    // passwordHash and internalRiskScore are simply never copied.
}
```

A senior nuance: separate request and response DTOs are often warranted, because what a client may send differs from what it may receive. A CreateAccountRequest might carry a password and validation annotations, while an AccountResponse never carries the password. Keeping these separate prevents mass-assignment vulnerabilities, where a client sneaks a field like isAdmin into a request that binds directly to an entity. The entity-to-DTO conversion belongs in the service layer, not the controller, so the controller stays a thin adapter.

