sections.s2_technical:
You define views as marker interfaces, often nested, where one view can extend another to inherit its fields. For example a Summary view, a Detail view that extends Summary, and an Admin view that extends Detail. This hierarchy means a field tagged Summary also appears in Detail and Admin.

```java
public class Views {
    public interface Summary {}
    public interface Detail extends Summary {}   // includes Summary fields
    public interface Admin extends Detail {}      // includes Detail + Summary fields
}
```

You annotate each field with the view it belongs to. A field tagged Summary appears in all three views; a field tagged Admin appears only in the admin view.

```java
public class AccountView {
    @JsonView(Views.Summary.class) private String accountId;
    @JsonView(Views.Summary.class) private BigDecimal balance;
    @JsonView(Views.Detail.class)  private String branchCode;
    @JsonView(Views.Admin.class)   private int internalRiskScore; // admin only
}
```

On the controller you activate a view with JsonView, and Jackson serialises only the matching fields.

```java
@GetMapping("/accounts/{id}/summary")
@JsonView(Views.Summary.class)         // only Summary-tagged fields
public AccountView summary(@PathVariable String id) { return service.get(id); }

@GetMapping("/accounts/{id}/admin")
@JsonView(Views.Admin.class)           // Summary + Detail + Admin fields
public AccountView admin(@PathVariable String id) { return service.get(id); }
```

For a dynamic choice at runtime, for example when the view depends on the caller's role rather than the endpoint, you wrap the object in a MappingJacksonValue and call setSerializationView with the chosen view class.

```java
MappingJacksonValue wrapper = new MappingJacksonValue(account);
wrapper.setSerializationView(user.isAdmin() ? Views.Admin.class : Views.Summary.class);
return wrapper; // Jackson applies the chosen view
```

The benefit is reducing DTO explosion. Without views, you might create AccountSummaryResponse, AccountDetailResponse, and AccountAdminResponse, three classes that are mostly the same fields, plus three mappers. With JsonView, one class and tagging produce all three subsets, which is less code to maintain when the fields overlap heavily.

However, JsonView has limits, and a dedicated DTO is better in several cases. When the shapes differ structurally, for example one endpoint nests objects differently or renames fields, views cannot express that because they only include or exclude existing fields. When there are computed values specific to one view, a DTO with that computed field is cleaner than squeezing logic into the model. When heavy transformation is needed, such as formatting or deriving fields, a DTO plus a mapper is clearer. And when a response combines multiple models, like an account plus its customer plus recent transactions, no single annotated class fits, so a purpose-built DTO is the right choice.

A senior nuance: JsonView is excellent for the narrow case of the same fields exposed in overlapping subsets, especially admin versus public views of one resource. But it couples the model to serialisation concerns through annotations, and it cannot reshape or combine data. So I reach for JsonView when the only difference between endpoints is which existing fields are visible, and I reach for dedicated DTOs when the structure, computed values, or data sources genuinely differ. Mixing them thoughtfully avoids both DTO explosion and the trap of forcing views to do transformation they cannot.

