    }

    public String audited(String actor, String from, BigDecimal amount) {
        String correlationId = MDC.get("correlationId");
        if (correlationId == null) correlationId = "CORR-" + System.nanoTime();

        // BEFORE — record the attempt no matter what happens next.
        auditService.append(AuditRecord.builder()
                .actor(actor).action("TRANSFER").target(from)
                .outcome("ATTEMPT").correlationId(correlationId)
                .timestamp(Instant.now()).build());

        try {
            transferService.transfer(actor, from, amount);
            // AFTER — success.
            auditService.append(record(actor, from, "SUCCESS", correlationId));
            return "APPROVED";
        } catch (SecurityException denied) {
            // AFTER — denied. THIS is the attack signal we must keep.
            auditService.append(record(actor, from, "DENIED", correlationId));
            return "DENIED";
        } catch (RuntimeException failed) {
            auditService.append(record(actor, from, "FAILURE", correlationId));
            return "FAILED";
        }
    }

    private AuditRecord record(String actor, String from, String outcome, String corr) {
        return AuditRecord.builder()
                .actor(actor).action("TRANSFER").target(from)
                .outcome(outcome).correlationId(corr)
                .timestamp(Instant.now()).build();
    }
}
```

sections.s2_technical:
The simplest approach uses the `org.json` library. To go from XML to JSON, you call `XML.toJSONObject(xmlString)` which returns a `JSONObject`, and then `toString` on it. To go the other way, you call `XML.toString(jsonObject)`. This is fast and needs no Java classes, but it is purely structural: it does not know your types, it can be ambiguous about whether a single repeated element is one object or an array, and it loses XML attributes-versus-elements distinctions in a lossy way.

```java
import org.json.JSONObject;
import org.json.XML;

String xml = "<payment><amount>50000</amount><currency>INR</currency></payment>";
JSONObject json = XML.toJSONObject(xml);     // {"payment":{"amount":50000,"currency":"INR"}}
String back = XML.toString(json);            // back to <payment>...</payment>
```

The robust approach uses Jackson. You define a Java class, for example a `Payment` with fields for amount and currency. You read XML into that class with an `XmlMapper`, and you write it out as JSON with an `ObjectMapper`. Because both map to the same object, the conversion is type-safe and controlled by annotations.

```java
XmlMapper xmlMapper = new XmlMapper();
Payment payment = xmlMapper.readValue(xml, Payment.class);        // XML → object

ObjectMapper jsonMapper = new ObjectMapper();
String json = jsonMapper.writeValueAsString(payment);             // object → JSON
```

Jackson annotations give you precise control. `@JacksonXmlRootElement(localName = "payment")` sets the XML root tag. `@JacksonXmlProperty(isAttribute = true)` marks a field as an XML attribute rather than a child element, which org.json cannot do cleanly. `@JacksonXmlElementWrapper` controls how lists are wrapped, solving the single-versus-array ambiguity that plagues the lightweight approach. These annotations mean the same `Payment` object serializes to clean, predictable JSON and clean, predictable XML.

For money fields you keep them as `BigDecimal`, never `double`, and Jackson serializes them faithfully in both formats. A senior nuance is that XML has no native number or boolean type — everything is text — so round-tripping through XML and back can turn a JSON number into a string unless you bind through a typed object. That is the strongest argument for the Jackson approach in a banking system: the typed `Payment` guarantees that amount stays a `BigDecimal` and currency stays an enum, no matter which format it arrived in.

Another nuance is namespaces and schema validation. Legacy banking XML often carries namespaces and must validate against an XSD. Jackson handles namespaces, and for strict validation you pair conversion with an XSD validator. When partners send JSON, you can validate against a JSON Schema instead. The conversion step and the validation step are separate concerns.

