sections.s2_technical:
gRPC starts from a contract written in a `.proto` file that defines services, their methods, and the message types. From this one file, the toolchain generates strongly typed client and server code in many languages, so a Java service and a Go service share the exact same contract and cannot drift out of sync. This code generation is a defining advantage: the types are enforced at compile time rather than hoped for at runtime.

```proto
syntax = "proto3";
service TransferService {
  rpc Transfer (TransferRequest) returns (TransferReply);
}
message TransferRequest { string from = 1; string to = 2; int64 amount_paise = 3; }
message TransferReply   { string transfer_id = 1; string status = 2; }
```

The wire format is Protocol Buffers, a compact binary encoding. Compared to JSON, protobuf is smaller because field names are not repeated on the wire — fields are identified by numeric tags — and values are packed in binary rather than text, so there is no parsing of strings into numbers. This reduces payload size and CPU cost of serialization, which matters enormously at high call volumes.

The transport is HTTP/2, which brings several benefits over HTTP/1.1. Multiplexing lets many calls share one connection concurrently without head-of-line blocking, so a service can have many in-flight requests on a single socket. Header compression reduces overhead. And HTTP/2 enables streaming, which REST over HTTP/1.1 does not naturally offer. gRPC uses this to support four call patterns: a simple request-response, server streaming, client streaming, and bidirectional streaming, so you can stream large result sets or live updates efficiently.

```java
// Generated blocking stub — a remote call looks like a local method call.
TransferServiceGrpc.TransferServiceBlockingStub stub =
    TransferServiceGrpc.newBlockingStub(channel);
TransferReply reply = stub.transfer(
    TransferRequest.newBuilder().setFrom("SAV2001").setTo("CUR3001")
        .setAmountPaise(5000000).build());
```

gRPC's strengths are performance and contract safety: the binary format and HTTP/2 make it fast and efficient, the generated stubs give end-to-end type safety across languages, and streaming is first-class. These make it excellent for the dense internal traffic between microservices, where both ends are yours and you control the clients.

Its weaknesses explain why it is not the default for public APIs. It is not human-readable, so you cannot just curl it or read a payload by eye, and debugging needs tools that understand protobuf. Browser support is limited; browsers cannot speak raw gRPC, so public web clients need gRPC-Web with a proxy. HTTP caching that REST enjoys via URLs is not available. And the ubiquity and low barrier of REST — any language, any tool, a simple JSON body — make REST far friendlier for external partners who must integrate quickly without special libraries.

When to choose which: use gRPC for internal microservice-to-microservice calls where latency and throughput matter, where you want strict typed contracts shared across polyglot services, and where streaming is useful, such as a transfer service calling a fraud service and an account service thousands of times a second. Use REST for public APIs, for anything browsers call directly, when human readability and easy debugging matter, and when wide client compatibility and HTTP caching are priorities. A senior nuance is that many banks run both: gRPC for the internal mesh and REST at the edge, often with a gateway that translates external REST calls into internal gRPC. In regulated environments the typed contract of gRPC also helps enforce exact message shapes between critical services, while REST at the edge keeps partner onboarding simple.

