sections.s2_technical:
Short polling is the simplest. The client sends a normal HTTP request every few seconds asking for new data, and the server responds immediately, usually with nothing new. It is trivial to implement and works everywhere, but it is wasteful: most responses are empty, it adds latency up to the polling interval, and at scale thousands of clients polling constantly hammer the server with mostly pointless requests. It is acceptable only when updates are rare or a few seconds of delay is fine.

Long polling improves efficiency. The client sends a request, and instead of replying immediately the server holds the request open until there is new data or a timeout. When data arrives, the server responds and the client immediately opens a new long-poll request. This removes most empty responses and delivers data closer to when it happens, but each update still costs a full HTTP request-response cycle with its headers and connection handling, and holding many open requests consumes server threads unless handled asynchronously.

```java
// Long polling: the server defers the response until data or timeout.
@GetMapping("/updates")
public DeferredResult<Update> poll() {
    DeferredResult<Update> result = new DeferredResult<>(30000L);  // 30s timeout
    updateQueue.register(result::setResult);   // completed when data arrives
    return result;
}
```

SSE, Server-Sent Events, is a true streaming push but one-way. The client opens one HTTP connection with `Accept: text/event-stream`, and the server keeps it open and writes events as they occur, each as a small text block. The browser's `EventSource` API consumes it and reconnects automatically if the connection drops, even resuming from the last event id. SSE is efficient because one connection carries many updates with minimal overhead, it runs over plain HTTP so it passes through proxies and works with standard infrastructure, and it is simple. Its limitation is that it is server-to-client only; the client cannot send data back on the same channel, and it carries text, not binary.

```java
// SSE: one connection, server pushes many events, client only listens.
@GetMapping(value = "/prices", produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE)
public SseEmitter prices() {
    SseEmitter emitter = new SseEmitter(0L);   // no timeout
    priceFeed.subscribe(price -> emitter.send(price));  // push as prices change
    return emitter;
}
```

WebSocket is full-duplex. It begins as an HTTP request with an `Upgrade: websocket` header; the server responds 101 Switching Protocols, and from then on both sides send messages freely over the single TCP connection with very low per-message overhead, carrying text or binary. It is ideal for truly interactive, two-way, low-latency scenarios. Its costs are that it is a different protocol needing WebSocket-aware infrastructure, it does not get HTTP caching, you manage connection lifecycle and reconnection yourself, and it holds a persistent connection per client, which you must plan for at scale.

The spectrum runs from least to most capable and from simplest to most complex. Short polling and long polling reuse ordinary HTTP request-response, SSE is a one-way HTTP stream, and WebSocket is a bidirectional upgraded channel. A senior nuance is to pick the least powerful tool that meets the need. If the server only pushes to the client, SSE is simpler and more robust than WebSocket, with free auto-reconnect and proxy friendliness. If both sides must talk interactively, WebSocket is required. Polling is a fallback for simple cases or environments where streaming is blocked. In banking, a stock ticker or a payment-status notification is one-way, so SSE fits; a live trading terminal or a support chat is two-way, so WebSocket fits; and a rarely changing status that tolerates delay can use polling.

