sections.s2_technical:
The defining difference is directionality. WebSocket is full-duplex: after an HTTP upgrade handshake, both client and server can send messages at any time over the same connection, independently and concurrently. SSE is half-duplex, one-way: the server streams events to the client, and the client cannot send anything back on that same channel; to send data the client makes a separate ordinary HTTP request.

This drives the use cases. If the interaction is genuinely two-way and interactive — a chat where both people type, a multiplayer game where inputs and state flow both ways, collaborative document editing where every keystroke syncs, or a trading terminal where orders go up and fills come down — you need WebSocket, because the client must push in real time. If the flow is only server-to-client — live notifications, a stock ticker, a monitoring dashboard, progress updates, a news feed — SSE is the better fit, because you do not need the client-to-server channel and SSE gives you the push for far less complexity.

SSE's practical advantages when one-way suffices are significant. It runs over plain HTTP, so it passes through proxies, load balancers, and firewalls that sometimes block WebSocket upgrades, and it reuses your existing HTTP authentication and infrastructure. The browser's `EventSource` API reconnects automatically if the connection drops and can resume from the last event id using the `Last-Event-ID` header, which you would have to build yourself with WebSocket. It is simple to implement on both ends.

```java
// SSE server — one-way push; the browser auto-reconnects on drop.
@GetMapping(value = "/notifications/{userId}", produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE)
public SseEmitter notifications(@PathVariable String userId) {
    SseEmitter emitter = new SseEmitter(0L);
    notificationHub.subscribe(userId, event -> emitter.send(event));
    return emitter;
}
```

```javascript
// SSE client — three lines, with automatic reconnection built in.
const es = new EventSource('/api/v1/notifications/rajesh');
es.onmessage = (e) => render(JSON.parse(e.data));
```

WebSocket's advantages are the things SSE cannot do: true bidirectional messaging, binary frames, and lower per-message overhead for very chatty two-way traffic. Its costs are that it needs WebSocket-aware infrastructure, you implement reconnection and heartbeats yourself, it does not benefit from HTTP caching, and you must manage the connection lifecycle carefully. SSE's limitations, beyond being one-way, are that it is text-only and that browsers historically limited the number of concurrent SSE connections per domain over HTTP/1.1, which HTTP/2 multiplexing mitigates.

A senior decision rule is to default to SSE when the requirement is purely server-push, and reach for WebSocket only when real-time client-to-server messaging is genuinely needed. Many teams reach for WebSocket reflexively for any real-time feature and then carry the extra complexity of reconnection logic and infrastructure for a feed that only ever pushes one way. In banking, notifications, tickers, and dashboards are overwhelmingly one-way and belong on SSE, while trading terminals, live chat support, and collaborative review tools are two-way and belong on WebSocket. If a mostly-one-way feature needs an occasional client action, that action can be a normal REST call alongside the SSE stream, keeping the streaming channel simple.

